first prompt
Write a beginner-friendly C++ console-based program called “Student Excuse Generator” that generates a random excuse for missing homework or assignments. The program should take the student’s name as input and store at least 10 excuse templates in a vector. Each template must include a placeholder like {name} that will be replaced with the user’s input.

However, intentionally introduce bugs into the program. The code should contain issues such as inconsistent placeholder usage (for example {Name} or {NAME} not being handled correctly), partial or missing replacement of {name} in some outputs, and randomness issues where excuses may repeat or behave unpredictably. The goal is to simulate a faulty implementation so that students can practice identifying and debugging errors effectively.
Second Prompt (Debugged)
Write a beginner-friendly C++ console-based program called “Student Excuse Generator” that generates a random excuse for missing homework or assignments. The program should take the student’s name as input, store at least 10 unique and creative excuse templates in a vector, and randomly select one excuse each time the program runs. It must correctly replace all variations of the placeholder such as {name}, {Name}, and {NAME} with the user’s input in every possible occurrence inside the selected string. The program should also ensure better randomness so that excuses do not repeat consecutively in back-to-back runs. Additionally, improve the program by adding a simple enhancement such as preventing immediate repetition of the same excuse or making the output more engaging with a formatted display. Provide a fully corrected, optimized, and reliable version of the program with clean logic and proper functionality.
